    "root_cause_category": "config_or_deploy_change",
    "explanation": "At 14:01 the deploy of checkout-api 2026.11.3 raised WORKER_CONCURRENCY from 8 to 32 (LOG-0001-0006, LOG-0001-0007). The orders-db client pool stayed at 20 connections (LOG-0001-0008). Before the deploy the pool was healthy, with 6 of 20 in use (LOG-0001-0003). After it, with four times as many workers competing for the same 20 connections, the pool went to 17 in use with 3 waiting, then filled up with the waiting queue growing to 70 (LOG-0001-0016, LOG-0001-0038). checkout-api then timed out after 3000 ms waiting for a connection and returned 503s (LOG-0001-0023, LOG-0001-0024), which tripped the p99 latency alert (LOG-0001-0036). The database itself was not under strain: only 20 of 200 sessions were in use and CPU was at 23% (LOG-0001-0025), so the queue is at checkout-api's own pool. The session-cache miss spike recovered by 14:04 (LOG-0001-0014), before the pool filled, so it is a side effect. The LB reports all six instances healthy (LOG-0001-0022), so hosts are not the problem. Rolling back the concurrency change, or sizing the pool to match the workers, should end the incident.",
